  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on expert-based anticipatory ratings rather than empirical post-implementation monitoring data.",
      "what": "An expert evaluation of 78 urban public transport measures across 10 European cities categorized interventions into four distinct operational profiles based on five performance dimensions. Using cluster analysis and principal component analysis, the study grouped interventions into high-performing transformative infrastructure, digital tools with strong user appeal but low system impact, administrative policies with low direct impact, and push policies that improve system performance at the expense of user satisfaction. Across all evaluated criteria, large-scale infrastructure interventions achieved the highest overall impact scores.",
      "why": "It replaces assumptions about transport intervention trade-offs with empirical classifications, showing that digital mobility tools primarily benefit users while systemic impacts depend on major physical infrastructure."
    }
  },
